<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matilda of Tuscany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1046">1046</a>–<a href="/years/1115">1115</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Matilda of Canossa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matilda of Canossa was margravine of Tuscany and ruler of wide lands in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>, Reggio, Mantua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>. A firm supporter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a>, she hosted the meeting at Canossa in 1077 where Emperor Henry IV did penance before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII</a>. After her death in 1115 many of her cities organised themselves as communes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany.jpg" alt="Matilda of Tuscany" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Donizo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mathilde_und_Hugo_von_Cluny_als_F%C3%BCrsprecher_Heinrichs_IV._(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Matilda of Canossa, called la Gran Contessa, was born around 1046, the youngest of three children of Boniface and Beatrice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, who had married in June 1037. Beatrice was the niece and foster daughter of Empress Gisela of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a>. Around 1055 Matilda was betrothed to her stepbrother Godfrey the Hunchback, and by 1069 she is clearly recorded as his wife. She bore a daughter in 1071 who died within weeks. She then repudiated her husband, a decision that ensured her independence. Her mother Beatrice trained her for rule by holding court with her and encouraging her to issue charters as countess and duchess.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After her mother's death Matilda took over her inheritance, though under Salic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> law Emperor Henry IV would have been the legal heir. She made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canossa-castle">Canossa Castle</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio Emilia</a>, the centre of domains that included present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and Tuscany. Pope Gregory VII excommunicated Henry IV in 1076, and in January 1077 Henry made his penitential walk before Canossa Castle and was received back into the Church. From 1080, when conflict with Henry resumed, Matilda put all her military and material resources at the service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. Her court sheltered many refugees and became a cultural centre.</p><p>Between 1081 and 1098 the disputes with Henry put her rule in crisis. A coalition with the southern German dukes brought a turning point, and when Henry withdrew north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> in 1097 she regained many lost domains from autumn 1098. Childless, she adopted Guido Guerra of the Guidi family around 1099 and, in November 1102, renewed a donation of her property to the Apostolic See. She directed her last gifts to Polirone Abbey, where she chose to be buried. According to Donizo, Henry V crowned her Imperial Vicar and Vice-Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at Bianello Castle in May 1111.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Matilda died on 24 July 1115, and the House of Canossa became extinct; popes and emperors fought over the Terre Matildiche, as her domains were called, well into the thirteenth century. The struggle between regnum and sacerdotium changed the Italian cities permanently, giving them room to escape foreign rule and develop as communes. Donizo's chronicle, called the Vita Mathildis, written between 1111 and 1115, is the main source for her life. In 1630 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a> had her body moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where she was the first woman buried in Saint Peter's Basilica. The Istituto Superiore di Studi Matildici, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1977, studies the Canossa family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matilda of Tuscany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its rise as a self-governing city followed the death of <b>Matilda of Canossa</b>, Margravine of Tuscany, in 1115.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The basilica is the burial place of 91 popes, along with Queen Christina of Sweden, Countess <b>Matilda of Tuscany</b> and the exiled Stuarts, and Benedict XVI was interred there on 5 January 2023.</blockquote><a href="/work/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ferrara was held by <b>Matilda of Canossa</b>, who besieged it in 1101 to put down a revolt.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-ferrara">Commune of Ferrara</a></li><li><blockquote>Mantua had been a centre of <b>Matilda of Canossa's</b> lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-mantua">Commune of Mantua</a></li><li><blockquote>Modena had belonged to the lands of <b>Matilda of Canossa</b>, and its cathedral was begun in 1099 by the architect Lanfranco with sculpture by Wiligelmo.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-modena">Commune of Modena</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1027 the house of Canossa held the march; <b>Countess Matilda</b> (ruled 1076–1115) hosted the meeting at Canossa in 1077 where Emperor Henry IV sought Pope Gregory VII's forgiveness, and fought for the papacy for decades.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-tuscany">March of Tuscany</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mantua. Died in Bondeno di Roncore, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.93,10.84&amp;year=1090" aria-label="Bondeno di Roncore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Matilda of Tuscany"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5652 3706h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5652 3706h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=44.93,10.84&amp;year=1090">What was here in 1090? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
